{
  "name": "How to Plan a Bathroom Refresh for a Foreclosed Property",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Revitalizing a foreclosed property's bathroom can be a smart move, but you need a solid plan. First things first, get a comprehensive inspection done. Document every detail, especially any existing damage, with photos. This initial step is crucial for defining a written scope of work. Next, sequence your project intelligently. Any major structural, plumbing, electrical, or water-intrusion problems must be tackled and resolved *before* you even start thinking about the fun stuff like new tile, fixtures, paint, and trim. This prevents headaches and costly rework down the line. Remember, foreclosed properties often hold secrets, so be prepared for discovery work that might pop up after demolition. Build in a contingency of about 15-25% for both time and budget to handle potential permit delays or unexpected contractor scheduling. And for anything beyond surface-level work, especially if it involves moving fixtures or changing electrical/plumbing, getting permits is non-negotiable. Don't skip that step!",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
